<Article
  title="Flip 7 Action Cards: Freeze, Flip Three and Second Chance"
  description="What each action card does, who you can target, and the edge cases that cause arguments."
  game="flip-7"
  gameTitle="Flip 7"
  category="Rules"
  publishDate="2026-08-09"
>
  <p>
    Nine of the 94 cards in Flip 7 are action cards, and they're responsible for
    roughly 100% of the rules arguments. The cards themselves are simple. The
    targeting is where groups go wrong.
  </p>

  <KeyTakeaway answer="Action cards resolve immediately when dealt, must be given to a player still in the round, and can be aimed at yourself. They never count toward your seven numbers and never bust you." />

  <h2>The Universal Rules</h2>

  <p>
    Three rules apply to all three action cards, and knowing them resolves most
    disputes before they start:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>They resolve the moment they're dealt.</strong> You don't hold an action card to play later — it takes effect immediately.</li>
    <li><strong>The target must still be in the round.</strong> You can't Freeze someone who already stayed or busted.</li>
    <li><strong>You are a legal target.</strong> Nothing stops you giving a card to yourself, and sometimes that's the correct play.</li>
  </ul>

  <InfoBox type="rule" title="Action cards can't bust you">
    Only a duplicate number card busts you. Being dealt a second Freeze in the
    same round is fine — it's not a duplicate in the sense that matters.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>Freeze</h2>

  <p>
    Give Freeze to any player still in the round. That player immediately
    <strong>stays</strong>: they stop taking cards and bank whatever is in front
    of them right now.
  </p>

  <p>
    The tactical read is not "who is winning" — it's who is about to get greedy.
    Freezing a player on 40 points is only good if they were about to push to 60.
    Freezing the player sitting on six unique numbers, one card away from a
    Flip 7, is the strongest use of the card in the game.
  </p>

  <InfoBox type="tip" title="Freezing yourself is not a joke play">
    If you've built a strong row and you know the deck is thick with your
    numbers, taking the Freeze yourself banks it safely. You were going to have
    to stay eventually — this lets you do it and deny nothing to nobody.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>Flip Three</h2>

  <p>
    The target immediately flips <strong>three cards, one at a time</strong>. They
    have no choice in the matter and no decision to make between cards.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>If they <strong>bust</strong> on the first or second card, it stops there. The remaining flips don't happen.</li>
    <li>If they hit <strong>Flip 7</strong> partway through, the round ends immediately.</li>
    <li>If an action card comes up during the three, it resolves as normal — then the remaining flips continue.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Flip Three is the aggressive card. Against a player with five or six unique
    high numbers, three forced cards is close to a death sentence. Against a
    player who just started their row, you may be handing them a Flip 7.
  </p>

  <InfoBox type="warning" title="Don't Flip Three the player with an empty row">
    Early in a round, forcing three cards on someone is a gift, not an attack —
    they can barely bust and you've fast-tracked them toward the bonus. Save it
    for a player who is already deep.
  </InfoBox>

  <h2>Second Chance</h2>

  <p>
    Second Chance sits in front of you as insurance. The first time you would
    bust, you discard the duplicate card <em>and</em> the Second Chance, and your
    turn continues as if nothing happened.
  </p>

  <p>
    The rule that catches people out: <strong>you can never hold two</strong>. If
    you're dealt a Second Chance while you already have one, you must give the new
    one to another player still in the round who doesn't have one. If every
    remaining player already has one, discard it.
  </p>

  <ComparisonTable title="Second Chance edge cases">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Situation</th>
          <th>What happens</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>You're dealt one and have none</td>
          <td>Keep it in front of you</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>You're dealt one and already have one</td>
          <td>Give it to an active player without one</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Everyone active already has one</td>
          <td>Discard the new card</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>You stay while holding an unused one</td>
          <td>It's discarded — it's worth no points</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>You bust while holding one</td>
          <td>You don't bust; discard the duplicate and the Second Chance</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </ComparisonTable>

  <h2>What If There's Nobody to Target?</h2>

  <p>
    If you're dealt an action card and you're the last player left in the round,
    you have to take it yourself. There's no option to discard it because the
    targeting is inconvenient — the card must resolve, and you're the only legal
    target.
  </p>

  <p>
    This produces the game's cruellest moment: you're alone with a beautiful row
    of six numbers, you'd love to stay, and the dealer hands you a Flip Three.
  </p>

  <InfoBox type="rule" title="Action cards and the seven">
    Worth repeating because it decides real games: <strong>action cards and
    modifiers do not count toward Flip 7</strong>. Only number cards do. Count
    numbers, not cards, when you're working out how close someone is.
  </InfoBox>

  <p>
    More disputed cases are answered in
    <a href="/games/flip-7/flip-7-rules-questions/">the Flip 7 rules FAQ</a>.
  </p>
</Article>
